  "summary": "The prior rounds correctly identify the decomposition axes and the modal-first sequencing trap, but both overarchitect the solution by proposing new abstractions (ActionDispatcher, BoardDataStore, PipelineOrchestrator, callback protocols) before exploiting the file's existing mechanical seams. A more pragmatic approach recognizes that the 4,298-line file has three extraction tiers of decreasing risk. Tier 1 (zero-risk, immediate): lines 84-481 are pure standalone functions with zero PipelineApp coupling—extract verbatim to tui_helpers.py with a single import change. The 10 modal classes (lines 569-1457, ~890 lines) and 3 pane/widget classes (lines 1458-1903, ~445 lines) are already self-contained class definitions whose only coupling to PipelineApp is through Textual's standard dismiss() return values and message posting—no callback protocol redesign is needed because Textual's existing message/dismiss pattern IS the protocol. Extract to tui_modals.py and tui_panes.py respectively. This alone removes ~1,500 lines with purely mechanical moves and no behavioral changes. Tier 2 (low-risk, high-value): split PipelineApp's action_* methods into two modules based on a simple mechanical test—does the method call @work or launch an async worker? If yes, it belongs in tui_workers.py as free async functions that accept a narrow typed interface (a Protocol with ~8 attributes: active_board, selected_feature, _current_features, _log_line, _refresh_board, _update_status_bar, config, _server_client) rather than the full PipelineApp. If no, it stays in PipelineApp as thin dispatch methods. This avoids the God Object concern from round 2 without requiring a full orchestrator abstraction—the Protocol constrains the surface area at the type level. Tier 3 (deferred): the _check_for_changes scaling concern is real but premature to solve in this refactor. The current implementation stats files for one board at a time with <100 features—this is microseconds of I/O. The right fix is directory mtime short-circuiting (check board dir mtime before individual files), which is a 5-line change independent of the structural refactor and should not gate it. Introducing watchdog/inotify adds a runtime dependency and platform-specific failure modes that are not justified by the current scale. The key insight the previous rounds missed: Textual's component model already provides the message-passing and state-sharing patterns needed for clean extraction. The modals already use dismiss() return values. The panes already use Textual Messages. PipelineApp already uses reactive attributes that Textual's watchers propagate. There is no need to invent new callback protocols or message buses—the framework already solved this. The refactor is mostly mechanical file splitting with import updates, not an architectural redesign."
